1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:05,540
Um, so millions of taxpayer dollars are wasted every single year on, you know,

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,660
government digital systems, systems that look perfect on paper but just completely fail in the wild.

3
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:13,660
Oh, absolutely. It happens all the time.

4
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:23,500
Right. So say you are running these digital systems in your ministry, and a vendor proudly tells your steering committee, "Hey, our platform is GovStack compliant at level two."

5
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,760
I mean, you probably feel a wave of relief, right?

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,440
Yeah, because it sounds like engineering precision.

7
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:33,700
Exactly. But does that actually mean the system works for your specific program?

8
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,580
Well, that is the ultimate question for any digital infrastructure project.

9
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:45,680
Right. So today's deep dive is all about exploring that critical difference between a system being merely, you know, set up and one that is

10
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:51,460
undeniably proven, and we are using GovStack testing, and the reference architecture as our guide.

11
00:00:51,840 --> 00:01:00,740
Right, because we naturally gravitate toward categories like level two because they feel comforting, they feel final, but compliance is really just a baseline,

12
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,400
not a guarantee of integration.

13
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,460
Wait, so if a vendor confidently puts a level two badge on the table,

14
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,580
I would assume that means the product is fully ready to go.

15
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:11,860
Are you telling me that badge isn't enough?

16
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:16,220
Well, it is a generic metric, not a specific one for your deployment.

17
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,840
GovStack testing evaluates a product in two main ways.

18
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:20,820
Okay. What are they?

19
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,360
First, the software provider conducts a self-assessment against functional requirements.

20
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:30,200
And second, automated tests are run against the product's published interfaces.

21
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:34,140
Basically, the digital doors and windows it uses to communicate.

22
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:39,880
Exactly. Passing these grants level one for partial compliance or level two for full compliance.

23
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:44,580
But this strictly evaluates the product in a vacuum, like isolated in a lab.

24
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:53,480
So it proves the software functions on its own, but the reference architecture requires external interfaces to actually talk to each other in the real world.

25
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:54,340
Exactly.

26
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:00,100
So that level two badge does not tell me how my specific country configured those connections.

27
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:10,220
That is the core issue. Connecting different interfaces across government departments is notoriously difficult because legacy systems are often fragmented, siloed, and

28
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:10,820
duplicated.

29
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:14,280
Right. They are just completely isolated from one another.

30
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:21,900
Yeah. So to prove a system actually works in this environment, you need one specific transparent check for every single configuration.

31
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:24,360
Okay, and what does that check actually look like?

32
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:32,560
It must define exactly what is being run, the published interface it tests, what counts as a pass, the exact result, and the date it was executed.

33
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:36,040
Right. So it, it's like buying a certified top-tier medical device.

34
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:40,440
The device itself might be level two compliant in a sterile testing facility.

35
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:43,080
Yeah, exactly. But if you just set it down in the room,

36
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:44,880
it is only set up.

37
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:49,800
It's not proven until you physically connect it, run the tests, and record the results with a timestamp.

38
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:53,620
Okay, so we are drawing a hard line between setup and proven here.

39
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:54,920
A very hard line.

40
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,580
If a configuration simply exists, it is only set up.

41
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:06,080
A digital building block is only truly proven when every single automated check has been run, passed, and recorded with its date.

42
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:08,320
Until you see that, you just have a setup.

43
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:16,120
I mean, this sounds logical in theory, but when a minister looks at an actual deployment blueprint, like the Progressa exchange,

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,380
they often just see a spreadsheet full of blank cells.

45
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:21,640
Progressa is a great example.

46
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:26,400
Right. And before the first run, every row simply says, "Not yet run."

47
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,020
I mean, why wouldn't seeing all those unchecked boxes make a project manager panic?

48
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:35,960
Well, it shouldn't, because it represents the honest gap between a design and a working proof.

49
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:39,160
It maps exactly what hurdles the system still needs to clear.

50
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:47,180
Right. Take check X.1, for example, which verifies if the system can accurately list members in the registration service, or check X.3,

51
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,460
which evaluates how the system handles permissions.

52
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:52,880
And X.3 isn't just testing if the system is turned on, right.

53
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:53,260
It is

54
00:03:53,880 --> 00:04:02,040
actively firing a request with a proper grant to see if it is answered, and then firing a request without proper permissions to ensure it gets refused.

55
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:07,660
Yes, exactly. It proves the security mechanism actively rejects bad requests in real time.

56
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:10,540
Oh, wow. Which is obviously vital for data security.

57
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:18,320
Then you have check X.4, which tests the entire once-only sequence from sign-in all the way to the register confirming the record.

58
00:04:18,959 --> 00:04:26,140
Ah, the once-only sequence. That's the ultimate goal for users, where they don't have to enter the same data five different times for five different departments.

59
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,020
Precisely.

60
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:31,720
So when the build is actually ready, the automated checks are run consecutively.

61
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:34,580
And the sheet fills up with results and dates.

62
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:40,960
Right. And if a check fails, it stays highly visible on the sheet until the developers fix the issue and it passes.

63
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:46,200
But most importantly, absolutely no results are ever edited by hand.

64
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:50,280
Wow. So it is entirely automated and tamper-proof.

65
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:51,100
Completely.

66
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:54,440
So if you are the one sitting across from that vendor,

67
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:01,120
the only defense you have against an unfinished system is demanding that timestamped sheet of past tests.

68
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:03,940
The data really just has to speak for itself.

69
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:04,580
Right.

70
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:09,960
Never settle for just a compliance level or a system that is merely set up.

71
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,320
Demand that it is proven. Exactly.

